  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe recent dry and mild weather will be coming to an end as we\nmove into the latter half of this week. We will have another day\nof highs in the 65-70 range with mostly sunny skies on Wednesday\nbefore a big change arrives. For tonight, there could be some fog\ndevelopment again, especially near lakes and rivers. Confidence is\nlow on how extensive it will become away from the lakes and\nrivers. Increasing high clouds may have an impact. \n\nA shortwave trough will move SE from the northern Plains and merge\nwith a large closed low over Ontario Wednesday night. Showers with\nthe associated surface cold front will begin to spread into the\narea around 00Z Thursday, and sweep through the area pretty\nquickly. The merger of the aforementioned troughs and surface low\nintensification over the Mid Atlantic will advect cold air deep\ninto the Southeast on Thursday, setting up a NW flow pattern for\nour area that persists for a few days. Cold temperatures aloft\nwill generate some instability for scattered to numerous showers\nThursday through Friday, with enhanced periods of precip coming\nwith embedded vort maxes rounding the closed upper low. The NW\nflow will enhance precip in the higher terrain of E TN and SW VA,\nand showers there will change over to snow showers on Thursday\nnight. Light snow accumulations are possible, but impacts in\npopulated areas should be minor with temperatures only borderline\nfor accumulating snow. Some flurries in the northern TN Valley and\nlower elevations of SW VA cannot be ruled out, but with no\naccumulation expected. Precip chances come to an end on Saturday\nas temperatures aloft warm and moisture exits with the departing\nupper low off the New England coast.\n\nBuilding high pressure across the TN-OH Valley region will bring\ndry conditions for early next week, with below normal temperatures.",
